rarer: siblings close by and in need; a rented home
times: Perhaps 1 in 5 people who move back near an ageing parent are later offered the family home or its upkeep (estimate)
scene: After Sunday lunch, {parent} puts the house keys on the table. {N} came home and stayed, {parent} says, so the house should go to {N}.
scene W: {N} knows the old rule: the house is shared, all the children equal. {parent} is offering to break that rule out of gratitude.
scene U: {N} thinks about what the house would mean: the roof, the bills, {parent} still in the back room, and how the others will hear it.
scene B: The house is worth more than {N} could save in a lifetime. {parent} is offering it freely, and the family will notice.
scene R: {N} sees {parent} trying hard not to cry, and the whole childhood of the house rushes back at once.
scene G: {N} came home like a root returning to its own ground. Now {parent} wants to hand over the ground itself.
scene gone: {parent} is not here to hand over the keys. The house stands quiet, the family asks {N} to decide, and {N} can almost hear {parent} in the kitchen.
